# The Dockerfile ends in `COPY . .`, which without this file copies
# backend/.env — real provider API keys — into an image layer, where
# they survive every later change and travel with the image if it is
# ever pushed or shared. Configuration reaches the container through
# the environment (docker-compose `environment:`/`env_file:`, Render's
# env var groups), never through a baked-in file.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Runtime data, not source. `uploads/` is 66MB of locally stored asset
# files and is gitignored, so a Render build (which builds from git)
# never has it anyway — but a local `docker build` would otherwise copy
# every uploaded PDF into the image. It is also the reason a build can
# fail outright here: OneDrive keeps unsynced files as placeholders,
# which the daemon cannot read ("invalid file request").
uploads/
data/
*.db
*.log

# Build/test artifacts: never useful at runtime, and they invalidate
# the layer cache on every local test run.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
